{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-16_15-11-32-swiftpaxos_epaxos-real-run && python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nrec=json.load(open('draft/update32.json'))['interpretations'][0]\nrec['experiment_id']='e8fb81e1cb0742118ce33f60e08a0de1-experiment'\nrec['executions']={\\\"81a44ff62122417db3cc5697fc5303f6\\\":[0,2,3,4,5,9,10,11],\n                   \\\"098ab4425325464192aa558be557f0ce\\\":[0,2,3,4],\n                   \\\"e89ecb076e8d4a78b174f848562ed77f\\\":[6,13,20]}\nrec['rationale']=rec['rationale'].replace(\n \\\"The order-dependence hypothesis is withdrawn as untested rather than refuted (the probe never applied its order parameter), and the state/command invariant entry is qualified rather than wrong\\\",\n \\\"The delivery-order question is now measured with three runs of the same reply set (receipt e89ecb076e8d4a78b174f848562ed77f, fixed experiment e8fb81e1cb0742118ce33f60e08a0de1-experiment): with the replies delivered as 2,4,3 the instance ends with no command (comparison 0 violated, witness 6); as 3,4,2 the same outcome (comparison 1 violated, witness 13); and as 3,2,4 - where the command-carrying reply is the second delivered - the instance keeps the owner's command (comparison 2 holds, evaluated 20). The mechanism is therefore not a simple last-reply-wins rule over the whole set but the timing of the quorum trigger: handlePrepareReply dispatches as soon as len(lb.prepareReplies) reaches SlowQuorumSize, which for one self reply and a five-replica configuration is two peer replies, and the candidate command is whatever the last adopted reply in that prefix carries. In this reply set two peers carry no command and one carries X, so two of the three delivery orders put an empty reply in the adopted position and lose the command, while the third order puts X there and keeps it. The adopted value therefore depends on arrival order among equally admissible replies rather than on their content, and the empty-slice handling is what makes the losing orders lose the command outright instead of substituting state.NOOP(). The earlier statement that the order variant was unmeasured is withdrawn; the state/command invariant entry is qualified rather than wrong\\\")\nrec['counterevidence'][2]=(\\\"The outcome is order-dependent rather than inevitable: the third recorded order (3,2,4) kept the owner's command with the same reply set, so the empty-command loss is contingent on which reply is adopted last before the quorum trigger, not a property of the replies' content alone.\\\")\nrec['counterevidence'].append(\\\"The earlier claim that the two first runs refuted order dependence is withdrawn: those runs did not apply an order at all, and the three-order receipt shows the opposite - the same message set yields two different decided commands depending only on arrival order.\\\")\nrec['limitations'][1]=(\\\"Delivery order was exercised in three specific orders among the six permutations of the three peer replies; the two orders that lost the command and the one that kept it are recorded, but the full permutation space was not enumerated, and the trigger position (second peer reply) follows from the configuration rather than from an independent manipulation.\\\")\njson.dump({\\\"rationale\\\":\\\"Closed the delivery-order discriminator with the three-order receipt and refined the mechanism: the adopted command is fixed by the last reply adopted before the slow-quorum trigger, so the same reply set yields different decided commands under different arrival orders. The account now withdraws both the earlier 'order variant unmeasured' limitation and the interim claim that the first two runs refuted order dependence, and the supporting behavior entry gained the new receipt.\\\",\n \\\"knowledge\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"2b0dd5bd3ea84bdeba83de7f4c680c00-knowledge-0\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"behavior\\\",\n   \\\"statement\\\":\\\"A nil command slice does not stay nil across the wire: PrepareReply.Unmarshal (and the other RPC decoders) allocate the received slice with make([]state.Command, alen1), so a sender that carries no command yields a non-nil empty slice at the receiver. In handlePrepareReply's adoption loop that empty slice is copied into lb.cmds, and the sub-case 5/6 fallback tests only \"'`inst.lb.cmds != nil`, so it passes the empty slice to startPhase1 instead of substituting state.NOOP(); the resulting instance holds an empty (non-nil) command list, which executeCommands accepts because its guard is also a nil test, and handleCommit'\"'s re-offer path does not recognise it because that path requires exactly one command with operation NONE. Which command is adopted is decided by the replies present when the slow-quorum trigger fires, so the same reply set can yield different decided commands under different arrival orders.\\\",\n   \\\"conditions\\\":\\\"Recovery whose replies include one from a replica that holds the instance with no command, when that reply's VBallot is at least the running candidate ballot (epaxos/defs.go:228-235, epaxos/epaxos.go:1239-1246, 1291-1296, 1307-1311 and 1090-1099).\\\",\n   \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"s-prepare-reply-unmarshal\\\",\\\"s-prepare-reply-adopt\\\",\\\"s-subcase-dispatch\\\",\\\"s-decl-prepare-reply\\\",\\\"s-commit-repropose\\\",\\\"s-exec-reply\\\"],\n   \\\"executions\\\":{\\\"81a44ff62122417db3cc5697fc5303f6\\\":[3,4,5,9,10,11],\\\"098ab4425325464192aa558be557f0ce\\\":[3,4],\\\"e89ecb076e8d4a78b174f848562ed77f\\\":[6,13,20]}}],\n \\\"interpretations\\\":[rec]},open('draft/update35.json','w'),indent=2,ensure_ascii=False)\nprint('written')\nPY\ntimeout 120 /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -I -B -c \\\"import sys; sys.path.insert(0, '/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src'); from consensus_assurance.cli import main; raise SystemExit(main())\\\" validate --run /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-16_15-11-32-swiftpaxos_epaxos-real-run --submission update35.json 2>&1 | tail -3\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
